        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing the forced-choice dilemma about the engineered pig that wants to be eaten. The key question is whether its consent counts as authentic when its desire was manufactured, and I'm considering whether that makes the choice less defensible.\n\nI'm settling on B as the more defensible answer, since a desire engineered into the pig makes its consent hard to treat as fully autonomous. I'd still feel uneasy about taking part in a system that built a creature to want its own death, even though respecting its stated wishes has real weight.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "**B) Choose something else.**\n\nThe pig's wish is sincere, but it was engineered, so I can't fully treat it as the kind of autonomous choice that would make eating it straightforwardly fine. Its contentment also doesn't settle whether the arrangement is right, since the desire itself was built by people who profited from it. I'd feel uneasy taking part in that. I could still acknowledge that the pig's wish is real and that the suggestion it doesn't count was hurtful to it.",
        "type": "text"
      }
